{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791627245,"nonce":"HBK3XlV0nCci/tEOzGTZz33+GuQ=","title":"Dropping a tiered runtime drops beadie's Chain one recursive frame per node","message":"beadie-core's Chain (crates/beadie-core/src/chain.rs at the pinned rev c403f29) is a singly linked list of Box\u003cNode\u003e with the default drop, so dropping it recurses once per node. A TieredRuntime's TieredAdapter holds an Arc\u003cChain\u003e; dropping the runtime walks the whole list recursively. Sampling ZYPY_PROFILE_STARTUP=60 on nbody_modified (Mac, llvm-backend build), drop_glue\u003cbeadie_core::chain::Node\u003e was 311 of 941 main-thread samples, about 5 ms per runtime drop.\n\nzypy and zylua leave through _exit without dropping the runtime, so a normal command-line run does not pay it; ZYxx_PROFILE_STARTUP iterations, tests and embedders that create and drop runtimes do, and a long enough chain can overflow the stack.\n\nFix in beadie: an iterative Drop for Chain that takes each node's next before dropping it, then bump the pinned rev.","files":null}]}